<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="locals" title="Locals">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        A handler's ordinary parameters copy the target's arguments. A local variable is not an
        argument. It has no position to take, so a parameter that wants one names it with
        <code>@Local</code>. Captures go last;
        <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and callbacks</a> has the rest of that rule.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["@Local on a trailing parameter"] --> B{"Was index written?"}
    B -->|yes| C["Read that slot; a described occupant of the wrong type is AW1050"]
    B -->|no| D{"Does the target carry debug information?"}
    D -->|no| E["Refused as AW1052"]
    D -->|yes| F["Search the variables live at this site"]
    F --> G["Name first, ordinal next, otherwise type alone"]
    C --> H["One load, emitted into the handler call"]
    G --> H
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="Which variable you get" id="strategies">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>What you write</td>
                <td>Which variable</td>
                <td>Needs debug info</td>
            </tr>
            <tr><td><code>@Local(index = 3)</code></td>
                <td>whatever occupies slot 3 here</td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>@Local(name = "total")</code></td>
                <td>the live one of that name</td><td>yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>@Local(ordinal = 1)</code></td>
                <td>the second of the type, in slot order</td><td>yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>@Local</code></td>
                <td>the one live variable of the type</td><td>yes</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Exactly one of the three elements is read, in the fixed order the table above lists:
            <code>index</code>, then <code>name</code>, then <code>ordinal</code>. Source order
            plays no part. It says nothing about the ones it dropped. Writing <code>index</code>
            beside a <code>name</code> quietly reads the slot.
            <a href="annotations.topic">The annotation reference</a> has every element.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Live here, not declared somewhere" id="scope">
        <p>
            A capture resolves once per site. That happens in the weaving moment of
            <a href="weaving-model.topic">the weaving model</a>. One declaration matching two sites
            can read two slots. Each is right where it stands.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Only what is live at that site">
                <p>
                    A variable declared further down, or one whose scope has already closed, does
                    not match — even though the class file still lists it.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> decides which sites a
                    declaration gets. <code>index</code> is the exception: it reads the slot even
                    where nothing is recorded as live there, since it names the slot outright rather
                    than searching the table for it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A slot is not a name">
                <p>
                    The compiler reuses a slot once a scope ends. One slot holding a
                    <code>String</code> at the top of a method can hold a <code>long</code> further
                    down, so <code>index</code> is right for one build of one target.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="How closely the type has to match">
                <p>
                    <code>name</code> and <code>index</code> take any reference for a reference
                    parameter. <code>ordinal</code> and a bare <code>@Local</code> are stricter:
                    their candidates are the live variables whose declared type equals the
                    parameter's exactly. A <code>CharSequence</code> parameter never finds a
                    <code>String</code> that way.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="One bad site costs the declaration">
                <p>
                    A site whose capture does not resolve withdraws the whole declaration, not that
                    one site. Each unresolved capture is reported on its own, so three of them
                    give you three messages instead of one.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Writing one back" id="mutable">
        <p>
            Java passes a parameter by value. Assigning to it changes the handler's own copy. To
            change the target's variable, write <code>mutable = true</code>. The parameter then has
            to be a carrier.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The carrier types">
                <p>
                    <code>LocalRef&lt;T&gt;</code> for any reference, and <code>LocalIntRef</code>
                    with its seven siblings for the primitives. The flag and the type are checked
                    against each other, so neither half of the intent can be written alone.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="What a carrier matches">
                <p>
                    What it holds, not its own type: a <code>LocalIntRef</code> resolves as an
                    <code>int</code>. <code>LocalRef</code> erases to <code>Object</code>, so
                    <code>ordinal</code> and a bare <code>@Local</code> look for a variable declared
                    exactly <code>Object</code>. Capture any other reference by name or by index.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="When the value lands">
                <p>
                    The write-back is emitted straight after the handler call and reads the carrier
                    once. A handler that stores into it later changes nothing. On the
                    <code>@Inject</code> path it runs before the cancellation branch, so a handler
                    that both writes and cancels gets both.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A redirect writes nothing back">
                <p>
                    No write-back is emitted for a <code>@Redirect</code> handler, so what it stores
                    into a carrier is discarded. Writing a local is what the
                    <code>@Inject</code> path does;
                    <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and callbacks</a> covers the
                    difference.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                A reference write-back is cast to the type the table recorded for the slot before
                the store, so a handler that put back the wrong type fails there with a
                <code>ClassCastException</code> naming that type, rather than somewhere later in
                the target's own code. A stripped target captured by <code>index</code> is the
                exception: with no table entry at that site, there is no recorded type to cast to.
                The cast is to <code>Object</code>, and a wrong type surfaces later, in the
                target's own code.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="When a capture is refused" id="refused">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The parameter is in the wrong place">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1040</code>. <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and
                    callbacks</a> has the ordering rule <code>@Local</code> adds a parameter to.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A <code>@Wrap</code> handler has less room than that: its
                    <code>Operation</code> parameter must be last. A capture placed after it is
                    <code>AW1062</code>; one placed before it is <code>AW1040</code> instead,
                    since it still leaves no parameter for the operation's own inputs. Either way,
                    delete the capture or use <code>@Inject</code>.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> covers the other shapes a
                    weave declares.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Nothing matched here">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1050</code>, and the message lists what is live at that site with its
                    types. Pick one of those, or move the injection to where the variable you meant
                    is live. A named slot that holds something else there reports the same code.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Several matched">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1051</code>: say which with <code>name</code> or with
                    <code>ordinal</code>. It comes only from resolution by type, where two
                    candidates and a coin flip would not be resolution.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="No usable local variable table here">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1052</code>: read the woven method's bytecode and capture by
                    <code>index</code> instead. No slot is inferred from the method's signature,
                    because a wrong slot reads a different value instead of failing.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Recompiling with <code>-g</code> is the usual fix. The table is built per
                    woven method body, though. A <code>-g</code> target whose one woven method
                    still has no placeable entry reports the same code, and <code>index</code> is
                    the only remedy there too.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <warning>
            <p>
                <code>mutable</code> and the parameter's type must agree. <code>mutable = true</code>
                on a plain parameter is <code>AW1053</code>: declare the carrier type. A carrier
                without the flag is <code>AW1054</code>: add <code>mutable = true</code>, or declare
                the parameter as the variable's own type if it only reads. Both arrive from the
                annotation processor at compile time and again from the engine.
                <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostics</a> says how to read a code.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

